               effectiveness and computational efficiency, their expressive
               power remains relatively unexplored, especially in connection to
               specific choices crucial in practice - e.g., carefully designed
               initialization distribution and potential use of complex numbers.
               In this paper, we show that combining MLPs with both real or
               complex linear diagonal recurrences leads to arbitrarily precise
               approximation of regular causal sequence-to-sequence maps. At the
               heart of our proof, we rely on a separation of concerns: the
               linear RNN provides a lossless encoding of the input sequence,
               and the MLP performs non-linear processing on this encoding.
               While we show that real diagonal linear recurrences are enough to
               achieve universality in this architecture, we prove that
               employing complex eigenvalues near unit disk - i.e., empirically
               the most successful strategy in S4 - greatly helps the RNN in
