  volume    =  27,
  number    =  12,
  pages     = "2443--2454",
  abstract  = "A long-standing goal in neuroscience is to understand how a
               circuit's form influences its function. Here, we reconstruct and
               analyze a synaptic wiring diagram of the larval zebrafish
               brainstem to predict key functional properties and validate them
               through comparison with physiological data. We identify modules
               of strongly connected neurons that turn out to be specialized for
               different behavioral functions, the control of eye and body
               movements. The eye movement module is further organized into two
               three-block cycles that support the positive feedback long
               hypothesized to underlie low-dimensional attractor dynamics in
               oculomotor control. We construct a neural network model based
               directly on the reconstructed wiring diagram that makes
               predictions for the cellular-resolution coding of eye position
