              Specifically, we test the hypothesis that the modular structure of
              brain networks fosters a dual mode of communication balancing
              diffusion—passive signal broadcasting—and routing—selective
              transmission via efficient paths. To this end, we introduce the
              relative diffusion score (RDS), a measure to quantify the
              proportional capacity for network communication via diffusion
              versus routing. We examined the interplay between RDS and
              connectome architecture in 6 organisms spanning a wide range of
              spatial resolutions and connectivity mapping techniques—from the
              complete nervous system of the larval fly to the inter-areal human
              connectome. Our analyses establish multiple lines of evidence
              suggesting that connectomes may be universally organised to
