  journal  = "Neuron",
  volume   =  82,
  number   =  4,
  pages    = "925--938",
  abstract = "To signal the onset of salient sensory features or execute
              well-timed motor sequences, neuronal circuits must transform
              streams of incoming spike trains into precisely timed firing. To
              address the efficiency and fidelity with which neurons can perform
              such computations, we developed a theory to characterize the
              capacity of feedforward networks to generate desired spike
              sequences. We find the maximum number of desired output spikes a
              neuron can implement to be 0.1-0.3 per synapse. We further present
              a biologically plausible learning rule that allows feedforward and
              recurrent networks to learn multiple mappings between inputs and
              desired spike sequences. We apply this framework to reconstruct
              synaptic weights from spiking activity and study the precision
              with which the temporal structure of ongoing behavior can be
