  abstract = "The idea that there is a fundamental cortical circuit that
              performs canonical computations remains compelling though far from
              proven. Here we review evidence for two canonical operations
              within sensory cortical areas: a feedforward computation of
              selectivity; and a recurrent computation of gain in which, given
              sufficiently strong external input, perhaps from multiple sources,
              intracortical input largely, but not completely, cancels this
              external input. This operation leads to many characteristic
              cortical nonlinearities in integrating multiple stimuli. The
              cortical computation must combine such local processing with
