               depolarisation of the lamina extracellular space subtracts away
               the standing background signal from the photoreceptor terminals.
               6. During dark adaptation the faster lamina mechanism can be
               superimposed upon slower photoreceptor processes (Fig. 9). 7. A
               comparison of our findings with studies of higher order neurons
               of the compound eye suggests that peripheral adaptation
               mechanisms play an important role in determining the response of
               the entire visual system. 8. The peripheral light adaptation
               processes of fly and dragonfly are similar, and the
               intensity/response functions of retinula cells and LMC's resemble
               those of vertebrate cones and bipolar cells respectively (Fig.
               11). We propose that this analogy has a functional basis. Both
               vertebrate and invertebrate systems use a ‘log
