  number   =  6,
  pages    = "1165--1177",
  abstract = "Many animals rely on visual motion detection for survival. Motion
              information is extracted from spatiotemporal intensity patterns on
              the retina, a paradigmatic neural computation. A phenomenological
              model, the Hassenstein-Reichardt correlator (HRC), relates visual
              inputs to neural activity and behavioral responses to motion, but
              the circuits that implement this computation remain unknown. By
              using cell-type speciﬁc genetic silencing, minimal motion stimuli,
              and in vivo calcium imaging, we examine two critical HRC inputs.
              These two pathways respond preferentially to light and dark moving
              edges. We demonstrate that these pathways perform overlapping but
              complementary subsets of the computations underlying the HRC. A
